---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro'
import { WhoPays } from '../../components/islands/WhoPays'
import { Top1TaxShare } from '../../components/islands/Top1TaxShare'
import { PayrollBill } from '../../components/islands/PayrollBill'
import { incomeGroups, revenue, vintageOf } from '../../data'

const sections = [
  { id: 'what-a-household-earns', label: 'What a household earns' },
  { id: 'the-spread', label: 'The spread' },
  { id: 'a-century-of-brackets', label: 'A century of brackets' },
  { id: 'statutory-vs-effective', label: 'Statutory is not effective' },
  { id: 'who-pays', label: 'Who pays the income tax' },
  { id: 'the-bill-you-do-not-see', label: 'The bill you do not see' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Households | US federal finance"
  description="Household income, inequality, and what people actually pay in tax."
  sections={sections}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 2 of 3</p>
  <h1>Households</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    What a household earns, how far the spread runs, and what the tax bill actually looks
    like once payroll tax is counted.
  </p>

  {sections.slice(0, 4).map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}><h2>{s.label}</h2><p class="num">Not built yet.</p></section>
  ))}

  <section id="who-pays">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 5</span></p>
    <h2>Who pays the income tax</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      In tax year 2023 the top 1% earned 20.6% of adjusted gross income and paid 38.4% of
      federal individual income tax.
      This is the individual income tax only.
      Outside the top decile, payroll tax is the larger bill.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Top 1%: 20.6% of AGI, 38.4% of the tax, a 26.3% average rate. Top 10%: 47.6% and
      70.5%. The bottom half paid 3.3% of the tax at a 3.7% average rate.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="The top 1% earned 20.6% of adjusted gross income and paid 38.4% of federal individual income tax in tax year 2023. The bottom half paid 3.3% of the tax. AGI share is not published for the Top 5%, Top 25% and Bottom 50% groups."
      title="Share of AGI and share of federal individual income tax paid, by income percentile group, tax year 2023"
      source={incomeGroups._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(incomeGroups._meta)}
      xUnit="Percent"
      yUnit="Percentile group of tax units"
      note="This chart counts the federal individual income tax only, and excludes refundable credits, which overstates the effective rate at the bottom. The six groups are nested — Top 1% sits inside Top 5%, inside Top 10%, and so on — so they overlap and do not sum to 100, which is why nothing here is stacked."
    >
      <WhoPays client:visible rows={incomeGroups.data.groups} />
    </Figure>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Share of federal individual income tax paid by the top 1% in the five published tax years: 33.2% in 2001, 38.8% in 2019, 45.8% in 2021, 40.4% in 2022 and 38.4% in 2023."
      title="Share of federal individual income tax paid by the top 1%, published years only"
      source={incomeGroups._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(incomeGroups._meta)}
      xUnit="Tax year"
      yUnit="Percent of income tax paid"
      note="Five published years, not an annual series. The 2001-to-2019 span is unobserved by this series."
    >
      <Top1TaxShare client:visible rows={incomeGroups.data.top1_tax_share_history} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Concentration of tax paid tracks concentration of income, and the tax-share column alone misleads about who pays the most versus who earns the most.
      CBO's longer series has the top 1% share of income before transfers and taxes going from 9% in 1979 to 18% in 2022, shown in <a href="#the-spread">the spread</a> above.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Refundable credits are excluded from the IRS series, which overstates the effective rate at the bottom.
      Tax year 2023 is the latest IRS year, and it breaks this site's FY1995 to FY2025 convention.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-bill-you-do-not-see">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 6</span></p>
    <h2>The bill you do not see</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Payroll tax raised 5.76% of GDP and 33.4% of all federal revenue in FY2025, and for
      every household outside the top decile it is the larger of the two bills. The chart
      in Section 5 counts none of it.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      FY2025: payroll tax 5.76% of GDP and 33.4% of total federal revenue, against
      individual income tax at 8.75% of GDP and 50.7% of revenue.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Payroll tax and individual income tax, each as a share of GDP and as a share of total federal revenue, fiscal 1962 to fiscal 2025. In FY2025 payroll tax was 5.76% of GDP and 33.4% of all federal revenue."
      title="Payroll tax and individual income tax, share of GDP and share of total revenue, FY1962-FY2025"
      source={revenue._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(revenue._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="Percent of GDP, or percent of total federal revenue"
      note="This figure is on fiscal years, while sections 1 through 5 are on calendar and tax years — FY2025 began in October 2024. Payroll tax funds Social Security and Medicare and is capped above a wage threshold, so its incidence is not the income tax's. Section 4's chart of CBO average federal tax rates does include payroll tax; this chart shows its scale as a share of federal revenue, which Section 4 does not."
    >
      <PayrollBill client:visible rows={revenue.data} />
    </Figure>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 7</span></p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      These limits are load bearing, not decorative. Read them before drawing conclusions
      from the charts above.
    </p>

    <p class="prose">
      <strong>1. Income tax only.</strong> Section 5 counts the federal individual income tax only. Section 6 shows the payroll bill it omits; excise, corporate, and state and local taxes are outside this route entirely.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>2. The Gini index here is for families, not households.</strong> The household series runs higher, about 0.47 to 0.49. The family series is used because it runs cleanly back to 1947.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>3. Statutory is not effective.</strong> A top marginal rate applies only to the slice of income above its threshold. Deductions, credits and separately-taxed capital gains all sit between the statutory rate and what anyone pays.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>4. Distributional data is by tax unit, not by person or by household</strong> — and not consistently across sources. The IRS counts returns filed, CBO counts households, and Census counts families. A married couple filing jointly is one tax unit, one household and one family, but a household of unrelated adults is several tax units. The shares on this route cannot be added across those sources.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      <strong>5. The dates break the site's convention.</strong> The Government route runs FY1995 to FY2025. This route reads IRS tax year 2023, Census/FRED calendar 2024 and CBO calendar 2022, because those are the latest each publishes. Refundable credits are excluded from the IRS series, which overstates the effective rate at the bottom.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
